Tree removal in Greater Cleveland typically costs between **$200 and $2,500**, depending on the size of the tree, where it's located on your property, and how complicated the job is. Most residential removals in NE Ohio fall between $450 and $1,100. Here's a straight breakdown of what you'll pay and why.

## Price Ranges by Tree Size

Size is the biggest driver of cost. Bigger trees mean more time, more crew, heavier equipment, and more debris to haul.

**Small trees (under 30 ft) — $200 to $450**
Think ornamental trees, young maples, small fruit trees. These are straightforward one-crew jobs that usually take a couple of hours.

**Medium trees (30–60 ft) — $450 to $900**
Most backyard maples, ashes, and oaks in NE Ohio fall here. Budget for half a day and factor in stump grinding if you want a clean yard.

**Large trees (60–80 ft) — $900 to $1,500**
Mature oaks, cottonwoods, large silver maples. These jobs require more crew, often a bucket truck or crane, and take a full day.

**Very large or complex trees — $1,500 to $2,500+**
Trees over 80 feet, trees leaning over a house, trees with major rot, or trees in tight access situations. The complexity, not just the size, is what drives cost here.

## What Else Affects the Price

**Location on your property.** A tree in an open yard costs less than one hanging over your roof or packed against a fence. Tight access means more hand work and more risk — both of which add time and cost.

**Tree health.** A dead or rotting tree is unpredictable. Brittle wood can break unexpectedly, which slows the job and requires more caution. You'll often pay a small premium for dead tree removal.

**Stump grinding.** Removal doesn't automatically include the stump. Grinding runs $100–$300 depending on diameter and how many stumps you have. Most homeowners add it — a leftover stump is an eyesore and a tripping hazard.

**Debris hauling.** Some companies leave the wood; others haul everything away. At Big Creek, we haul everything — logs, brush, chips — and leave zero trace.

**Emergency and after-hours work.** Storm damage at 2am costs more than a scheduled removal on a Tuesday morning. Emergency response typically runs 50–100% above standard rates.

## Why Cleveland-Area Prices Vary So Much

You'll get quotes ranging wildly on the same tree. The gap usually comes down to three things: insurance coverage, equipment quality, and experience. An unlicensed crew with a chainsaw and a pickup truck will quote you less. They'll also leave you holding the liability if something goes wrong on your property.

In Ohio, any damage a tree causes during removal is the contractor's responsibility — if they're properly insured. If they're not, it's yours. Always ask for proof of liability insurance before anyone touches a tree on your property.

